// ---- views -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A view is two words, {pointer, length}: the first two words of a vector's header, which is why a
// view of a whole vector needs no record of its own - it is the header (rt_view). A view of part of
// one does need a record, and the two must be told apart when a view is dropped, because a record is
// freed and a header is its owner's. The address says which: every block the allocator hands out,
// and every static object the compiler emits, starts at a multiple of 16, and a view record is the
// second word onward of a 32-byte block, so its address is 8 more than a multiple of 16. Nothing is
// read to decide, so a view may be dropped after the vector it was taken from.
fn rt_view_record() -> u64 {
    return rt_alloc(32) + 8;
